    "\n",
    "### Prediction Of Helmet Adaption After Mandate For Underaged Users\n",
    "#### Objective\n",
    "based on this qualitive analysis a quantative objective is structured as follow:\n",
    "\n",
    "This analysis predicts how helmet use is likely to spread among 12- to 17-year-old e-bike and fatbike riders once helmets become mandatory. Qualitative data on youth attitudes toward helmets were used to set upper limits on adoption under three scenarios. The resulting projections provide the basis for estimating how many severe and fatal head injuries the mandate could prevent.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Approach\n",
    "\n",
    "The projections used an asymptotic growth model using parametres based of the evaluation of the 2023 Dutch light moped (snorfiets) mandate and surveys of youth attitudes. In each scenario, adoption starts from the current voluntary helmet use of 0.4% and rises month by month with an assumption addaption rate of k = 0.4 toward a scenario-specific ceiling:\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Best case:** Adoption follows the pattern observed after the light moped mandate, where 67% of continuing riders reported \"always\" wearing a helmet.\n",
    "* **Expected case:** The best-case ceiling is reduced to account for social pressure. In the surveys, 48.3% of young people feared comments from others about wearing a helmet, and 46.9% valued others' opinions on the matter. Applying a 48% reduction to the 67% ceiling yields a maximum compliance of 34.8%.\n",
    "* **Worst case:** Underage riders lack a legal transport alternative. After the light moped mandate, cars absorbed more than half of the displaced trips, an option unavailable to this age group. Widespread avoidance behaviour was therefore assumed, such as tuning e-bikes to 32–37 km/h, which caps compliance at 15%. \n",
    "\n",
    "#### Results\n",
    "\n",
    "Compliance varies considerably across the three scenarios. The code below presents the projected helmet use at Month 1, Month 6 and Month 12, showing how social resistance and avoidance behaviour hold back the adoption curve relative to the best case. \n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
